1. **HTML Structure**: Ensure your table has a structure that supports sorting. Each header cell (`<th>`) should have a unique identifier or class to indicate it is sortable.

2. **CSS Styling**: Add some CSS to style the headers and provide visual feedback when they are being dragged or sorted.

3. **JavaScript Implementation**: Use the HTML5 Drag and Drop API to handle the sorting logic. Here is a basic example:

```html
<table class="table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th draggable="true" id="header1">Name</th>
      <th draggable="true" id="header2">Age</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Alice</td>
      <td>25</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Bob</td>
      <td>30</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<script>
  const headers = document.querySelectorAll('th[draggable="true"]');
  const rows = document.querySelectorAll('tbody tr');

  headers.forEach(header => {
    header.addEventListener('dragstart', (e) => {
      e.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', header.id);
    });
  });

  rows.forEach(row => {
    row.addEventListener('dragover', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
    });

    row.addEventListener('drop', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const draggedHeaderId = e.dataTransfer.getData('text/plain');
      const draggedHeader = document.getElementById(draggedHeaderId);
      const newOrder = Array.from(rows).map((row, index) => index);
      const oldOrder = Array.from(headers).map((header, index) => index);

      // Reorder the rows based on the new order
      rows.forEach((row, index) => {
        row.style.order = newOrder[index];
      });

      // Reorder the headers based on the new order
      headers.forEach((header, index) => {
        header.style.order = oldOrder[index];
      });
    });
  });
</script>
```

This code sets up the table to be sortable by dragging and dropping the headers. When a header is dragged, its ID is stored in the data transfer. When a row is dropped, the rows and headers are reordered based on the new position.

For a more robust solution, you can also consider using a library like SortableJS, which simplifies the process and handles edge cases better. However, if you strictly want to avoid external libraries, the HTML5 Drag and Drop API is a viable option.

